// expect: 101
// A FEEDBACK PRIOR SHADOWS THE LOCAL THAT SHADOWS IT. Inside the recurrence
// `let prev = 100` rebinds `prev`, so `prev + x` is 101. The program
// answers 1: lowering keeps the priors on one global stack keyed by name and
// consults it before the frame's own locals, so `prev` reads the prior,
// which is 0 on the first tick (measured 2026-09-25; `mentl check` is
// clean).
//
// Retires when lowering resolves a reference through its binder edge
// rather than by name (Hβ.resolve.reference-is-an-edge).
fn step(x) =
  ((prev) => {
    let prev = 100
    prev + x
  }) <~ delay(1)

fn main() = step(1)
